{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/strict-action-and-revision.md", "content": "# Strict-action discipline after failed submissions

In costly ARC evaluator streams, every turn must contain exactly one allowed JSON action. A failed submission is evidence against that complete candidate, but binary feedback does not identify which part is wrong. Record the exact candidate/rule attempted and do not resend it unchanged. Re-derive a materially different candidate from the demonstration constraints or spend a demo request only when it can distinguish live hypotheses.

Avoid non-puzzle tool calls during the active evaluator loop: they do not provide new constraints and increase the risk of violating the one-action contract. Before responding, check current demo/attempt counts, ensure the selected action is still allowed, and emit only its JSON object on the final line.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/strict-action-and-revision.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/unchanged-demo-and-failed-hypothesis.md", "content": "# Unchanged demos and hypothesis revision

An input-output pair with no changes is an active constraint, not a non-example. A salient sparse motif may be inert or lack the feature that triggers the demonstrated operation. Compare every demo for changed and preserved coordinates, including clusters that look similar but receive different treatment.

After a wrong submission, reject the complete hypothesis and revisit the transformation family; don't make cosmetic coordinate edits. Separate trigger, propagation geometry, selected extent, and handling of original markers. A single demo that extends a diagonal may support a ray hypothesis, but unchanged sparse clusters elsewhere are counterevidence to blindly extending every similar-looking track. Request more only when an example can distinguish specific alternatives; otherwise form a materially revised candidate from the full evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/unchanged-demo-and-failed-hypothesis.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/binary-feedback-and-mixed-demos.md", "content": "# Binary feedback and heterogeneous demos

A demo stream may vary grid dimensions and colors while illustrating a shared transformation. Compare each pair as a complete constraint set: record all changed and preserved cells, boundaries, propagation direction, and where filling stops. Do not assume unrelated palette changes mean a different task.

After an incorrect submission, treat the result as evidence against the submitted rule/grid. State internally what assumption failed, revisit demo differences, and change the candidate meaningfully before trying again. Avoid submitting an identical grid repeatedly. Binary feedback doesn't identify the correct cell, so use the existing demonstrations to narrow alternatives; spend remaining demo requests only when a new example can discriminate among live hypotheses.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/binary-feedback-and-mixed-demos.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/session-lessons-protocol-and-fill-rule.md", "content": "# Protocol discipline and avoiding speculative fill rules

Continual ARC tasks require one valid action on every active turn, even after an interruption. A prose status report is not a valid substitute; when the instance remains active, choose among submit, request_demos, or finish according to the live counts and end-state rules. Do not make unrelated tool calls in the evaluator loop: they consume interaction opportunities without providing puzzle evidence.

After each demo, compare all changed and preserved cells before taking another costly action. In fill/color tasks, do not assume the output is a filled bounding rectangle or the complement of a flood-filled exterior. Those are competing hypotheses that need to explain every demo, including holes, boundaries, marker cells, and untouched regions. Consider directional propagation or selectively filled regions only when supported by cellwise evidence. An incorrect submission should prompt a meaningful rule revision, not a cosmetic redraw.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/session-lessons-protocol-and-fill-rule.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/panel-extraction-and-dimension-check.md", "content": "# Panel extraction and output-dimension validation

When demonstrations have unusually wide/tall inputs and much smaller outputs, inspect the entire layout before assuming a cellwise transformation. Look for a separator line/column and distinct panels on either side. Across demos, compare output dimensions to the panel dimensions: if the output consistently matches one panel, the task likely extracts or transforms that panel while discarding the other, separator, or framing. Normalize colors by role and test candidate panel selection against every example.

After inferring the selected panel, determine its transformation independently from the discarded region. Do not let random-looking cells elsewhere in the input distract from a compact output panel. Apply the same spatial crop/projection to the test input, then map colors using the demos. Validate the resulting row count and each row width against the inferred output dimensions before emitting JSON. Incorrect feedback may stem from a wrong selection rule, a wrong transformation, or simply malformed output dimensions; distinguish these failure classes before spending another attempt.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/panel-extraction-and-dimension-check.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/stripe-sequence-validation.md", "content": "# Uniform stripe sequence tasks: validation and revision

For grids made of full-width, single-color rows, collapse each grid to a 1-D sequence of row colors before inferring the transformation. Compare input and output sequences position by position, including leading and trailing background rows and every gap between visible stripes. Check whether output preserves anchor rows, repeats a motif, extends a sequence, or uses a phase determined by the first/last marker. Do not infer a 2-D periodic tiling merely because other tasks in the broader benchmark use diagonal traces; select the transformation family supported by the current demos.

